{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790260948,"nonce":"Kzefqrp2K4XtLhH76t73/DgYNpY=","target":"c545df4b000f39811784f38e2ab467f79bd7373fac13a2207901f8d8e363a47c","message":"A slot typed from the constructors (here Int or nil) is read through the class\nchain after a blind store put a string into the class: the class sits in a\ntable whose element type is not known, and the store goes through it by name.\nThe read goes the general way (may_hide sees the blind store) but its value is\nstill coerced to the slot's type, so it prints a large integer. Same output on\nmain dea6d08b and with the l2 field-read work, in every tier; lua5.4 prints\nthe string.\n\n    local C = {}\n    C.__index = C\n    local function new(v) return setmetatable({f = v}, C) end\n    local o = new(nil)\n    local p = new(2)\n    local reg = {}\n    reg[1] = C\n    print(o.f)\n    reg[1].f = \"via alias\"\n    print(o.f)             -- lua5.4: via alias\n    table.insert(reg, 1, \"x\")\n\nWithout the table.insert (reg's elements stay typed as C) it prints correctly.\nThe same garbage shows for a store through `poke(reg[1], \"f\", v)`, a load'ed\nchunk with C in its environment, and a coroutine body storing into C, all\nafter reg's elements lost their type. The read's type should take in what a\nblind store may have put behind it. Related to a8fd3a3.","files":null}]}